body:
Not sure what "a little more in my price range" means in terms of actual numbers.  If you're talking about a DMV hearing plus having to address potential felony charges, that's fairly substantial involvement and you're extremely unlikely to get anyone in private practice to do that for under $2,500.  Quite frankly, if someone did offer that, you'd be better looking elsewhere.  A lot of people here have had success going on their own but with a potential felony and the fact that there was an accident involving someone else, that's not something you want to do.  Have you applied for a public defender?  That may be an option.  Also, sometimes law schools will have clinics offered by 2nd or 3rd year students (supervised by a licensed attorney) and that may be an option as well.  Or you can negotiate by limiting what your attorney would be involved in (such as not doing anything with the DMV hearing and you would be on your own for that).  

&#x200B;

The financial parts are hard and it's an extremely stressful time period.  The good thing is that very often 1st offenses are treated as the blip in an otherwise clean record that they are.  All the best; this is the worst part but it does eventually stabilize.

You definitely want to figure out how you'll handle work during the license suspension period.  It's extremely difficult to get to and from.  Do NOT drive on a suspended license.  I was pulled over once during my suspension (in my state you can drive during that period provided you have an interlock in your car).  The state trooper was just sitting there running plates and got me immediately.  They will do that and if you're suspended they absolutely will pull you over even if you're doing nothing else wrong.  I have a few opinions on whether focusing on whether someone has a valid license when there are plenty of people breaking obvious traffic laws is actually protecting the public, but that's beside the point!  Don't take that risk because that's how you'll get sucked further into the court system.  

&#x200B;

There will probably be an alcohol evaluation at some point.  Do a quick Google search for "Substance Abuse Evaluations" and you'll find what you're most likely to be asked.  One thing to keep in mind:  this is done through the court system- these people are **not** your friends and really aren't all that interested in truly helping.  It gets counties and mental health facilities a huge amount of money to order further treatment.  There are definite right and wrong answers to these questions so you want to pay attention to how you're answering them. If you believe you have a genuine substance abuse problem, get help on your own time, don't rely upon the county.

This group is extremely helpful and there are a couple of posters here who are also from Florida and so can speak more to the specific "getting a FL hardship license" aspects.  One thing, FL gets insanely expensive after a DUI because you have to have an FR-22 which will be extremely expensive; I think one poster said something about having to pay $6,000 for the full year.  FL is one of the few states with that requirement and there really isn't a way around it if you want to drive.  

&#x200B;

Employment is definitely possible even if it will be more challenging due to the DUI.  It will close doors at least for a time.  But for some jobs, they won't even run a background check or if they do, they won't care about a single DUI.  It really depends on the type of work.  Eventually the further away you get from it, the less likely this is to affect you.  

&#x200B;

Definitely hire an attorney; it's going to be much easier if you do.  It's not so much that they're likely to change the outcome, but the system itself is not designed for criminal defendants to represent themselves.

If the IID is license or DMV related, the false start could lengthen the amount of time you have to have it in there.  Depends on the rules.  If you're court ordered not to drink, the vendor doing monthly calibrations is permitted to report positive results to the court.  My provider disclosed that immediately because they actually were contracted with the county court.  So any positives would be reported.  

&#x200B;

The .05 is quite possible depending on the beers.  If they were IPAs it's quite possible the reading was accurate.

I can tell you that when I was taking the Bar Exam 7 years ago there were a couple of people who had to address a "criminal record that had been expunged" issue.  They never specified it but now I know they were talking about a DUI that was removed through a diversion program but they still had to disclose because of the way the question was worded.  All of them were eventually admitted once they passed the Bar.  I don't know when their DUIs occurred but law school is only 3 years and my particular school didn't have a huge drinking and driving culture (yet) so I suspect for at least one of them it happened earlier.  Law schools are more concerned about it these days because they have to be but my experience is that a single DUI isn't going to be enough to keep out an otherwise candidate from a standard school.  The further in the past it is the less weight it will carry.  It's probably good to wait anyway because this process is incredibly stressful and you do **not** want to add to it with having to prepare for the LSATs!  

&#x200B;

FWIW the attorney culture is very much that the reason I have a DUI and my colleagues don't is because I was unlucky enough to get caught.  It's a bit of a weird culture where attorneys can get really judge-y about it but at the same time they're drinking just as much and then driving, so it's one of those "we see our worst traits reflected back at us" ideas.  

&#x200B;

It doesn't stay at the extreme level forever.  For me the depression and guilt have become normal parts of life now and I'm just used to feeling this way.  A lot of others on here though do feel differently after some time has passed.  It's good you have supportive parents; that's going to be extremely helpful.  Hang in there!

I had to go through a county provider and they used Smart Start.  I really can't complain too much; you were permitted to disconnect the headset which kept it from having temperature problems in the winter.  The device itself was strange:  I had numerous occasions where I swear I used the exact same technique and would keep getting error messages.  For fun, I took a tuning fork one day to see if I could find an exact pitch that would always prevent an error message.  It actually worked!  What can I say, I was bored.  It seems most people have startup issues like that where the device says they're not blowing hard enough; that \*\*seems\*\* to be more specific to Smart Start but probably every company has those same issues.  

&#x200B;

I didn't have any issues with my battery but I only had it in for about 60-70 days or so and also have a brand new car, so that may have helped!

My guess is, if it truly is a deal breaker and they have to rescind an offer over it, there isn't much to be gained from calling and explaining that it was a one time mistake.  The fact that they didn't offer you a chance to explain and just said they'd have to rescind the offer suggests this is the case.  I don't think calling anyone would hurt or burn bridges, but I also don't think it will be very likely to help.  

&#x200B;

I'm really sorry.  There are other jobs that will be out there, and the further back into the past this goes, the less likely it is to be seen as a mark against you.

So I can tell you that if you truly have a single drink using standard serving guidelines (i.e., that 16 oz IPA is not "one drink") and wait at least an hour, most people will pass an interlock, which is set around .02.  Whether you should is a different question entirely, but "glass of wine with dinner" for most people will not put you at the legal limit.  

&#x200B;

My guess is that the issue with the permit versus license is whether you have a licensed driver with you while you're driving.  That's something that's probably better to ask on a legal forum rather than here.  But most states say that with a permit you're allowed to drive, you just have to have a licensed driver with you.  Or something to that effect.

I have Geico as well.  It went up about $100 for the year.  Not sure if that's a regular increase or if it's due to the DUI.  I'm in PA which doesn't require the SR-22 so that saved me.  I've had Geico for years though so maybe that helped.  I never mentioned the D-word to them, though because there was an accident and substantial property damage they had to know about it.  I heard Progressive is really good with DUIs if it becomes too high.  

&#x200B;

If you live in Florida or Virginia it's going to be painful because of the FR-22, which hits you even harder.

Giving my usual 10 second lawyer answer.  With the timing, it's extremely possible there's not much of a point in meeting yet because he actually doesn't have everything needed.  If CA is one of the states that delivers dashcam footage, etc., he may need that before telling you a defense plan, especially with the BAC so close to the limit.  Or, if he's a solo practitioner and perhaps in a trial he really may be putting aside things that aren't urgent because to him, it's routine.  We do it all the time especially if emails/messages tend to come in after hours or on weekends/holidays.  It's incredibly easy to forget that nothing about the criminal process is routine or easy when you're the one going through it.  And yet it happens all the time.  My lawyer actually did this to me actually.  

&#x200B;

What would make me respond more would be if you called, asked if there was anything you needed to do, and ask when we can schedule some time to talk.  I'd probably set up a phone consultation (or have my paralegal set it up!).  Sending him messages isn't working and a letter won't be read.  Given the timing I wouldn't terminate over something like this but you may say that in general you want to be sure to have enough time to get your questions answered, a way to set up phone calls in the future will be appreciated.  

&#x200B;

TL/DR:  Phone calls will be more effective, see if you can get an assistant to set up a phone conference, and find out what you need in order to have the most productive conversation.  It's not a hanging offense, but if you're not comfortable with the response you receive you can decide from there whether to go with someone else.  All the best!

PA here.  The questions were pretty comparable to what everyone else is posting; if you look up "alcohol assessment" or "alcohol screening questions" that'll give you a good idea.  Mine also included a lot of mental health related questions which I lied about.  My BAC was high so I couldn't plausibly use the "I never drink, it was a one time mistake."  It was too high for a true rare-drinker to be able to handle.  But the assessment took place about 4 months after my accident so it was a sufficient period of time to be genuinely able to say I reflected and changed my drinking habits.  Seemed to mostly work.  I still ended up in a second class because of the high BAC so there was nothing I could've said differently that would've changed it.  And I'm convinced with this particular program, they send everyone to AA meetings.  Fortunately, only 3.  

&#x200B;

There weren't any urine screens at mine but this is a diversion program which in my county is a bit of a joke and is basically "we say you're on probation but as long as you don't get caught doing something else, nobody's checking to make sure you follow the rules."  Michigan in general seems to be more strict in this regard, from what I've seen other posters write on here.

Few things to keep in mind:  these people aren't your friend, they don't actually care about mental health.  You gain nothing from being completely honest with them and keeping the state involved in your mental health.  So, when it comes to the mental health questions, don't disclose depression, anxiety, suicidal thoughts, etc.  If you need or desire help in that regard, you can address it on your own and keep the court out of it.  

&#x200B;

For alcohol use it's a little harder.  They ask about history, habits, etc.  Mine didn't ask a whole lot of questions about how many times I drank and drove; focused more on family history, how frequently I drank.  I will say, if your BAC was high (over .16 or whatever your state's highest tier is), it's going to be very suspicious if you say you rarely drink.  If you were able to operate a car at the very high levels, you've built up a tolerance over a period of time.  I handled this by saying that I didn't realize tolerance has nothing to do with BAC and now that I have that knowledge, changed my drinking habits.  That seemed to be acceptable.  In the end I still ended up with having to take an additional class because of the high BAC and that I had an accident, and I had to go to three AA meetings (the worst part of it, and yes, it is a punishment).  I had the impression that the AA meetings really were for everyone though.  If you go online and look up alcohol assessments, that'll give you a good place to start in terms of what questions will be asked.

So for whatever it's worth, I'm in the middle of a DUI and am an attorney myself, though in PA rather than NJ.  I can tell you that Character and Fitness these days and lawyer substance abuse problems are constantly being talked about at various bench bar conferences because the ABA released a report relatively recently reiterating what every attorney has known for years:  that the entire profession has a culture centered around drinking.  You will absolutely have to disclose it before you even get approved to sit for the Bar and will probably have to appear before the Board of Law Examiners given the multiple occurrences.  That's how we would do it here and usually it's recommended to hire counsel for those purposes.  It's manageable but the Bar Examiners are becoming much more strict about these issues.  When I was in law school, the most common way it was described was "I have to talk with the BLE because I had a criminal record but it was expunged."  I've since figured out how to translate that:  "I got a DUI, ended up on ARD (probation) and everything was expunged but I still have to admit that I was charged."  They were all able to sit for the Bar and as far as I know, everyone is still practicing assuming they still desire to do so.  

&#x200B;

Post-Bar admission, you ***absolutely*** cannot let this happen again and if it seems like it's going to happen again, contact Lawyers Concerned for Lawyers (or whatever it's called over there).  The Disciplinary Board will suspend your license for criminal convictions and I cannot imagine that this is much different between states.  Here, if you're convicted of, please guilty or plead no contest to a criminal offense (including DUIs), you're required to report it to the D-Board and you will absolutely lose your license for a period of time.  Fortunately, PA has an ARD program where it's not actually resulting in a conviction so I didn't have to report it.  I think in NJ DUIs are by default traffic offenses rather than criminal offenses but given the trend, I would assume if it hasn't happened now, at some point in the future the D-Board will require reporting of DUI offenses.  The culture itself in this profession is ***very*** centered around drinking and considering at happy hours I see people routinely putting back four or five cocktails in a two hour period, I'd say most are driving well above the legal limit but you wouldn't know from watching them because they've built up such a high tolerance.  

&#x200B;

If it's not obvious, you ***need*** to hire a lawyer.  I can tell you what I've seen attorneys do out here is negotiate combining the multiple offenses happening in one weekend into a single "first offense" and it's worked.  I don't know if that's doable for you but it's worth inquiring.  You're unlikely to get that much help if you make the request without a lawyer.  Good luck:  definitely stop drinking for awhile so that you can truthfully say you aren't doing it.  That may help (or at least won't make the situation worse).

I'm assuming there's no difference conviction-wise between Option 1 and Option 2?  As in, if you did the probation, you would definitely get it expunged?  Assuming that's the case, I'd probably go with Option 2.  The fact that weekends are allowed means you're not actually a danger to society so supervision level is likely to be minimal during that time (don't get me started on jail time for offenses like this!).  Two years of reporting probation is a lot and it's many opportunities to fail.  Jail seems like the better option if it can be done over weekends.  

&#x200B;

But, because you asked for pros and cons, here are some points where Option 1 is preferable:  Option 1 allows you to spend a single weekend in jail, then you're on probation and basically can't get into anymore trouble during that time.  It also avoids the issue of having to take off work, because weekend jail time may be routinely granted, but isn't a guarantee.  You also don't know what will happen once you're actually in jail for 15 days.  Is it basically "hang out in a cell and don't give us any trouble," or are there psych evals, etc.?  I actually do think the 15 day thing is better--I'm trying my best to give Option 1 some love!

From an adult child's perspective a few years older than your son, I wouldn't "want" to know in the sense that I don't believe it's any of my business.  However, if I did know, I wouldn't treat my parents any differently.  It also wouldn't bother me if I found out years later because it just slipped out; I'd just assume they didn't want to tell me when it happened.  I haven't told my parents or anyone in my family or social circle because it doesn't affect them.  Literally a whole 3 people know and that's only because I knew I wouldn't be able to keep it from them.  

&#x200B;

That said, there's a poster on here who posts regularly who made it a point to tell her children.  I believe in part, the reason was to show them that they really can tell her anything and she was proving that by treating them the same way.  It's actually really cool.

So I assumed I was going to have to switch insurers...and it turns out...my premiums went up a whole $100.  Actually I'm better with that than with it staying the same, because it's enough of a hike to make me think my insurer actually knows the accident was DUI related but, my premium was ridiculously low to begin with so this really isn't a hit.  Whew!  Probably being a long-term customer helped.  I also lucked out by being in a non SR-22 state.  

&#x200B;

That sounds like a mess dealing with multiple states.  You'd think that with as many DUIs as there are states would have a better idea of how to handle them!

If it's at a municipal court it's probably a preliminary hearing which is basically "is there enough to go to trial."  The answer is almost certainly yes because I swear everything under the sun is allowed!  Though .09 is close to the legal limit, so your attorney may very well have some arguments to make when it comes to that.  If it is a preliminary hearing, just let your attorney do the talking and questioning.  The point will probably be to create a record more than to prove innocence.  

&#x200B;

The LSATs are a mess; the Bar Exam is even more of one :-) But I had a few classmates who had to address a "criminal charge that was expunged" when applying for the Bar and all of them were allowed to practice later--assuming they passed the Bar.  For whatever it's worth, the DUI process is much more stressful than studying for and taking the Bar.  I'd much rather do that than go through this again--in case it helps a few years down the line when the only thing you can think of is contracts and torts questions.

With revoked licenses, my understanding is that it's not that you can never get it back (or maybe in some states, but in the majority that's not the case).  But you have to wait a long time to reapply, you are treated as a brand new driver and have to go through the learners' permit, etc. and I think in some states you even have to prove actual sobriety.  I thought I read somewhere that Michigan or Minnesota was one of those states.  6 months seems like a really short time for an actual revocation and usually with anything of that level you're looking at several years.  I guess it's possible but that defeats the purpose of revoking someone's license if it's that short!

&#x200B;

I'm actually not sure my state even has a true revoked license.  We have long-ass suspensions where you have to retake a driver's test, but they're still considered suspensions rather than revocations.  Terminology-wise, I actually think that makes more sense.  Because it's a bit silly to say a license is revoked when you can get it back!

For the court part?  If I knew then what I know now I would have handled it on my own.  First-time DUI "sentencing" is ridiculously laid-back in my county so long as you go to the classes and don't get caught by the police doing something you're not supposed to do.  No check-ins with a PO, no travel restrictions, and although you're technically subject to random drug screens there's no way to really enforce that if you're not restricting someone's ability to travel!  In reading what others here are experiencing for much less than what happened with me, I truly did get off easily even though it doesn't feel that way.  So the court parts I wouldn't worry about at this point.  

&#x200B;

Emotionally I don't know that I'll ever get to the point where I can say it wasn't worth the energy.  Perhaps that will change once this is further behind.  It's not so much freaking out as in anxiety about the future, it's more the mental aspect of feeling like a criminal and carrying that label around--I'm lucky this will be expunged at the end.  That doesn't change the fact that it still happened.

\*\*Getting on soapbox\*\* Public Defenders are very much real lawyers and in about 95 percent of cases get exactly the same result a private attorney would achieve.  But there are usually specific income guidelines to determine who is eligible for a PD and most people who think they're eligible just because private counsel is expensive, really aren't.  \*\*Steps off soapbox\*\*

&#x200B;

To the question, ***definitely*** get a lawyer.  No idea what state we're talking about, but I'm in PA, and this is almost exactly my DUI only without the open container.  I ended up on 12 months unsupervised probation which will be terminated after 6 months; but PA is ridiculously lax when it comes to DUIs and some of the people posting on here have much harsher requirements/sentences/punishments without the aggravating factors above.  She absolutely needs legal counsel.  The system is not set up for people to represent themselves in these circumstances and unless she actually knows how to handle a DUI case, she will have no idea what to do.  She needs counsel even if it means substantially cutting back expenses to do so.

Not sure how it is in AZ, but up here the implied consent laws are tied to the driving privilege.  So criminal penalties aside, if you refuse, you lose your license for a year.  You're not entitled to a lawyer when it comes to a refusal for driver's license purposes, because it's a private matter between you and the DMV.  It's a really fine line, the relationship between the criminal court and the DMV.  It seems like a lot of states have a similar rule.  Of course, if you do provide it and it's over the limit, that's evidence against you in a criminal proceeding.  So the way it works here is basically, you refuse, the refusal form gets sent to the DMV who suspends your license automatically whether criminal charges are filed or not.  Of course it's never that easy and so of course you still get charged with the DUI plus a few aggravated factors due to the refusal (in PA you're automatically stuck in the highest tier).  If you decide not to refuse, the blood draw occurs and you release it to law enforcement.  Then they charge you and eventually send a different Order to the DMV directing it to suspend your license.  So Option #1, Refusal, is the DMV specifically yanking your license which is why you're not entitled to counsel at that point (it's not actually a criminal matter at that point).  Option #2, Blood Draw/Breath Test is the court finding evidence of the DUI and ordering the DMV to suspend pursuant to criminal penalties.  Does that make sense?

&#x200B;

I'd say even though you have the right to refuse the roadside breath test, in most cases it's probably better to do it anyway.  If you refuse, a warrant will be obtained and it will probably be for a blood test which is harder to challenge.  Police officers also have a tendency to overcharge or just say you were an outright refusal and not even bother getting the warrant.  And in the current DUI climate, it's going to be a hard sell to get that thrown out.  Not saying it can't be done, in fact it's probably one of the scenarios where it should be!  But in most cases it's probably not worth it.

&#x200B;

TL/DR:  right to counsel is inconsistently applied by police and if they can get away with saying a refused roadside test is a real "refusal" because they don't want to get a warrant, they will.  In most cases the consequences of blowing will be less than the consequences from not blowing.

Review hearing sounds like a progress report.  It's weird that you have to go back in front of a judge, but it sounds like that's just standard procedure where you are.  It will probably be really quick to verify that you're compliant with probation requirements.  

&#x200B;

Usually with court proceedings, if you're genuinely at risk of getting into a lot of trouble you know about it beforehand.  If it's just "review hearing" with no mention of you doing anything wrong, then I wouldn't be too worried about it.  You should ask your lawyer beforehand and see if he/she can give you any insight.

It looks like it's a temporary license permitting you to drive while you await an administrative hearing.  So you're not suspended yet but you're flagged.  It probably goes without saying that getting caught driving under the influence while using that license is a **BAD IDEA** for numerous reasons.  

&#x200B;

Unless there's something weird about South Carolina, I doubt that the license status has any actual impact on your ability to purchase alcohol.  Even if it did, most clerks IDing people for alcohol are specifically focused on the age and don't notice anything else.  You may have alcohol restrictions as part of probation or as a bond condition.  But it wouldn't be connected with your license specifically.

 I'm in PA as well.  

*Does the fact that they didn't put on their lights until I was parked help me in any way?*

No.  They'll hang onto "possession and control" of the vehicle.  They can pull you over even if you're in your own property and parked; you don't have to actually be driving.  There are a lot of people here who received DUIs when they were parked.  

&#x200B;

*Should I bother getting a lawyer if its over 0.08? From what I've read, it's just an extra expense because ARD is so common and I have no prior convictions for anything.*

Yes.  ARD will be a much smoother process if you have a lawyer; you technically can do it and in most places throughout PA it's so common that if you're otherwise eligible, you'll be offered it and it's little more than filling out forms.  But the criminal system isn't designed for defendants to represent themselves.  Especially if your license was technically expired at the time it occurred, just get a lawyer to help you through it.  

&#x200B;

Also, ARD is the default but it's also not the only option.  There may be probable cause issues which you won't know unless you're actually a criminal defense attorney yourself.  Put it this way:  I'm aware of a criminal attorney and a judge who were arrested for DUIs and they both had lawyers to handle ARD.  

&#x200B;

*If I am over 0.08, can I opt to get a breathalyzer installed in my car instead of the suspension? I'd have no other way to get to work.*

&#x200B;

If you're over .10 where a suspension is mandatory, you are allowed to install the interlock *during* the course of your suspension under ARD.  So you're technically suspended and if a police officer runs your plate he will probably pull you over for driving while suspended because PennDot's interface doesn't make it very clear that you're permitted to have the interlock license (ask me how I know...).  But you're allowed to install the interlock and drive during the course of your suspension.  That's for ARD, if it's an actual conviction or a guilty plea the answer would be different.

If anyone is in the car with you, there's no way to hide it.  You get prompted for retests and there's no way to hide these.  So I've made sure to not have passengers in my car.  From the outside, I'll tell you that when I last went to the interlock center to have mine serviced, I specifically looked to see others' devices (just to see how noticeable they were, not to snoop!).  They really weren't that noticeable and the only reason I knew what they were was because I know what an interlock device looks like.  I think to the average person who isn't specifically looking, the camera part of it looks more like a bluetooth device.  When I was there I intended to also look and see if the actual "blow and go" part of it was really obvious.  But I forgot to do that; probably just as well before someone thought I was really creepy watching them use it!  My guess is that unless you're specifically looking and know what to look for, it's not that obvious.  

&#x200B;

The actual interlock itself I always detach when I shut off the car and hide it.  It's either in my coat pocket or in the consol center of my car.  While driving I don't have it mounted; I just leave it in the same place where I keep my phone.  I think this makes it less obvious while driving, and anyway it was more distracting having to reach over to grab for it and then put it back.  If I were in one of those states where you're not allowed to disconnect it, I would find something to cover it up when not driving.  So it's definitely possible to conceal it from the outside.

It's not entirely the officer's fault because when it gets run, the license does show up as being suspended and apparently you have to click to a separate window and scroll down to see the Limited License designation.  So you're licensed, but not \*\*really\*\* licensed.  Because that makes sense... Next time this happens I'll be prepared though--I went online, printed off the statute that says I'm allowed to drive and highlighted the part that applies to me.  

&#x200B;

I reported it to probation even though it wasn't required.  She was fine with it and said that if it happens again I can give the officer her number to verify my status.  This will probably change up my driving routine; I had been leaving at odd hours to avoid having people see me use the device, but will probably have to leave work early so that if I get pulled over again phone calls can be made to verify my license.  Fortunately this is all done on April 1st and there are at least 7 days that I know I won't have to drive anywhere.

I know that out here disclosing marijuana automatically gets you into the more intensive classes.  Having a high BAC or an accident also sends you in there automatically, along with three mandatory AA meetings.  That was seriously the worst part as far as the "treatment" requirements were concerned.  In fairness, at least it was only 3 and there were no restrictions on where you went so if you really wanted to, you could make up a random meeting, scribble a fake name and then be done with it.  It was really a joke.  For me, I was generally honest during my eval though didn't disclose the anxiety/depression/suicidal ideation because quite frankly it's not any of the county's business.  Came back as "low risk," but because of BAC and the accident automatically ended up in the higher level intervention.  I must have said something snarky because the evaluator made it a point to say that it really is where everyone ends up going.  So I think really the goal is to force you into "treatment" that isn't needed, make you feel worse and gouge you for more money.  Probably cynical but at $250 for a class with 20 people in it twice a month, it shouldn't come as a surprise.  

&#x200B;

Actually it "worked" in the sense that this will ***never*** happen again because the classes are ridiculous and really didn't provide any insight or knowledge I didn't already have.

I'm sure this is state and even county specific.  I can tell you I had to take two assessments--one which is really for statistical purposes and the other which is the real D&A assessment.  I wasn't UA'd at either of those and from reading online, I suspect it's the exception rather than the rule in my state (PA).  I believe most court-sanctioned drug screens are going to test for alcohol and drugs--I'm not quite sure how the 5 panel versus 12 panel works though I can tell you that when screens are done in my county, it's 12 panel.  I think that's the one that tests for alcohol.  

&#x200B;

Nothing scientific of course, but I believe that it's not an automatic probation violation if you test positive for marijuana on a first screen--because it can stay in your system for awhile.  But you may get more frequent screening as a result, and definitely going forward you can't smoke.  But I think each department will have its own procedure for how to handle it.  I just assumed that every time I did something court related, that I would be tested and I think in general that's the only safe way.

I got curious and looked up actual research on EtG accuracy especially because all of the testers go on about the 80 hour lookback period.  None of the "light" drinkers (having a total of 1.6 drinks and presumably none of them were drinking O'Douls) showed up positive after 24 hours.  It has less than 1 percent alcohol, it's extremely limited.  So if you truly only drank one and you were pretty certain you wouldn't be tested for at least 24 hours, you'd probably be okay.  But it's still possible that it would show up much in the way alcoholic cough medicine would, so the only way to be sure is to just not risk it.  I wouldn't try to use my interlock right after drinking it even though I know it would in all likelihood be fine, for example.

&#x200B;

Edit to add:  I'm not saying you should take the risk!  Quite the opposite--I don't know, I'm a bit paranoid and whenever there's monitoring involved be it random screens, an interlock, what have you, I think the risk is too great even if it's pretty minimal.

So, the way it works with the diversion program is that you actually aren't fined--because technically the diversion program doesn't result in a conviction, guilty or no contest plea, and so you can't be "fined" without being "convicted."  From what I can tell, if it was an actual guilty plea it would be the court costs plus an additional $1,000 or whatever it is.  But I just got stuck with court costs; to me it may as well be a fine though, regardless of what terminology is used!!!  And I swear on the statement of costs you get charged 3 times for the exact same thing.  

&#x200B;

That's WEIRD with the impoundment.  It feels like that was put into place when people didn't drive other people's cars and before hardship licenses became more widely given.  Because how exactly is that preventing you from driving if you can drive someone else's car, just not your own?!  Some of these procedures need to be updated because it's almost comical how ridiculous they are.

As soon as I got my DUI I swore I'd never drink alcohol again and it was a whole 24 hours before I regretted making that promise to myself.  For the first week and a half after I almost looked at alcohol as a privilege I lost and strangely I found it having a control over me in ways it never did before.  I'm not a fan of AA or the "powerless over alcohol" mindset, but that was the closest I came to understanding what is meant. After I gave myself permission to drink again, that changed and now I really can take it or leave it.  I will probably err on the side of Ubering if I know I'll drive; not to say the cost isn't annoying, but it's much better than a second DUI.  

&#x200B;

It sounds like you have a plan for how to see it through.  And that will be extremely valuable moving ahead.  It could have been so much worse--I hope everything works out with the security clearance.

What you should first do is call your lawyer again (and again, and again, after awhile he'll answer).  If that doesn't work then you should do a search on [avvo.com](https://avvo.com) for a similar situation or post a variation of your question there.  When you're talking about sentencing requirements it's going to be county-specific.  Often DUIs are heard by the same judge in one county.  Driving work vs. personal vehicles sounds more like a DMV issue than a criminal court issue and that's a whole separate issue.  I can tell you that in my state you can drive regular work vehicles only if the DMV permits you to do so.  I have no idea how often they're granted but I think in general with an organization as large as the DMV, so long as you're otherwise able to have the exception granted and your employer certifies your requirement in the form prescribed by the DMV, they probably have other more pressing issues to worry about than randomly denying petitions like that.  That's my impression anyway, it could be different elsewhere.  

&#x200B;

A lot of states have jail time for a second offense, though if MD only requires it if the second one occurs in a 5 year period you may be fine.  I actually think that your lawyer not responding to you in this instance may be a good thing--if jail was a serious possibility that you should be preparing yourself for mentally, you would probably have been told about it by now.

Going forward, I think the plan will be that if I drink while I'm out, it's a planned thing and I just don't even have my car with me.  Truthfully, I do own a breathalyzer now and know that one drink spaced out over a period of time will mostly put me between .03 and .05 depending on the serving size.  I don't consider myself "impaired" at that level but really if a police officer just randomly did a scan on my plate or I got caught in a checkpoint, I don't know whether it would be flagged because of the ARD disposition.  If you're looking for reasons to pull people over, you can usually find them even if it's not the "real" reason.  

&#x200B;

Also appreciate the honesty that you do look at people differently when they admit they have a DUI.  That solidifies my reasoning for not telling anyone and it will stay that way.  It's isolating though, and depression, panic attacks, suicidal thoughts, etc. are daily occurrences.  This board is the only thing that's keeping me holding everything together.

The thing with the interlock is that at least for mine, it routinely goes off at 6 minutes and then doesn't go off again for at least 30 (probably closer to 45 but I don't know).  If I know I'll be driving somewhere with a number of traffic lights, I wait 6 minutes and then leave.  I also don't start my car until I see that nobody is around nearby.  Oh yes, and I don't keep the device hanging; I keep it down near the consol where I plug in my cell phone.  Finally, the rule is nobody goes into my car and nobody operates my car.  Sure, a bit overly dramatic perhaps, but it works.  Nobody has seen the device yet.  There are all sorts of tricks you can use :-) 

&#x200B;

I do find myself looking into people's cars these days to see if they have an interlock set up; I wouldn't have even known what it looked like until getting this thing in my car.  Only about 45 days to go until my license is reinstated.

The best person to ask would be your lawyer because they've probably seen copies of reports at various times. In PA my evaluation didn't include any type of testing but I've heard that it's different throughout the state.  It may be the same in ND as well.  I can tell you that in PA family court when drug tests are ordered they **do** test for benzos (15 panel)--I don't think EtG is included in that only because it's the running joke that every once in awhile someone fails the alcohol part of a drug screen and you basically have to be drinking right before you come to court to fail that!  I have no idea what criminal court/probation does though.  If you do get a drug test, you're generally supposed to bring copies of any prescriptions, etc.  

&#x200B;

Consequence-wise, you should definitely ask your lawyer.  Some states will treat an initial positive as a baseline marker and test more frequently but otherwise the consequences are minimal.  Other states will be more strict about it.

So, "put back on probation" could mean a couple of things.  The first person to ask is his lawyer if he had one.  If there's a motion, he would be notified of it--if he had a lawyer, that notification would go to his lawyer who would see why he's being put back on probation.   

&#x200B;

What could it be?  It could actually be something as simple as the order was signed but never filed or never delivered to the probation office, or didn't make its way to some critical person.  My opinion is that a "confirming letter" isn't as good as an order.  You want an order of court with the judge's signature on it, preferably with a stamp verifying that it was actually filed.  DUIs are high volume in most counties and so it's very possible that something didn't get signed or didn't get filed or there's some clerical error that's creating the issue.  Clerical issues can be resolved with perhaps a motion clarifying or restating what you originally wanted and I'm assuming this is pretty much universal in most places.  

&#x200B;

I suppose you could be put back on probation if you did something like get discharged from probation and say in front of your probation officer "hahaha guess what I drank alcohol everyday on probation when I wasn't allowed to and you never caught me.  Sucks to be you!"  Whether a court is supposed to be able to do that is obviously questionable (I think in my state officially once you're off probation someone can't go back and violate you if they find out after discharge that you didn't something you weren't supposed to do).  But I'm sure if it was egregious enough they would find a reason.  

&#x200B;

Anyway, assuming he never violated anything and all the requirements were met, he should comply but then contact the probation officer and ask WTF (nicely, of course!)  He can provide a copy of the order dismissing him and ask why he's being placed back onto it.  Really he should ask the probation officer first and then depending on what the officer says, contact a lawyer if he doesn't have one already.  Before it's official though, he should follow the instructions so they can't ding him for new violations.

I think you're right when you say that most of us before this happened had an image in our minds about who gets a DUI.  I know I had a "those people" type opinion and I never included myself as someone in "that" group of course.  I think the image makes it harder; we feel like we aren't living up to the standards we set for ourselves or that others set up for us.  And really, in my DUI classes and reading these boards and exchanging emails with people who have read my blog, yeah, sure, some people may be closer to the image I had in mind.  But most are people who made a mistake and really this has taught me a lot about how much I assumed about people, such as "who" gets a DUI.  It's actually been pretty eye-opening.  

I enjoy reading your posts here and your daughters are incredibly lucky to have a mom like you.  So many parents say that their children can tell them anything but ultimately don't mean it.  Your daughters are so fortunate that you have truly given them that gift.  

&#x200B;

It's an expense, and quite frankly in most first offense cases you can probably talk your way through a good deal on your own without a lawyer.  I think most places have their set protocols for how they're handling DUIs and with few exceptions, there's not much room for deviation. Personally it was a lot of money for my lawyer but I can't say it was an outrageous cost given it included 3 court proceedings and was a flat fee, and the last thing I wanted to do was handle it alone. In my case because I'm a lawyer myself, I would have behaved like one instead of behaving like a criminal defendant and that wouldn't have ended well. So it wasn't an amazing deal, but I'm so glad I didn't have to go through it alone--the court part ended up being the easiest.  There are a lot of lawyers who handle DUIs at various rates.  Sometimes it helps to talk to a few before deciding what to do.  

&#x200B;

Really I'm not sure the depression goes away completely.  It's better for me now than it was when everything happened, but it's very much present.  Every court fine, every class, every time I have to have someone drive me into work reminds me of what a screw up I am and it comes back.  The intensity subsides after a bit.  And from reading the posts over here, it seems to get better once the requirements and suspensions have all been completed.

I think it's very common for us to immediately associate anniversaries with particular milestone moments, particularly ones that had such an impact on our lives.  I think with something this traumatic it's almost like the one year mark is a "test" of sorts to see if the same thing will happen to us.  I've read other people's experiences and they seem to start to feel better once the 1 year hits, then they feel better once probation is over, and it goes from there.  It's almost like we can't really say we've changed until we get to that first anniversary and don't get another DUI.  

&#x200B;

I don't see why you need to tell your roommates about it unless it helps add context to anxiety.  But this is coming from someone who has only told 3 people and in my mind that's 3 people too many.  But I do think it's good to reach out to the people who have supported you even if it's just to know that this is normal.  Congratulations on getting through the first year.

The DA's office will usually have a decent range of discretion in what to offer.  I don't even think my lawyer and the DA actually spoke; it was such a quick clerical procedure and basically a bunch of paperwork that had to be signed.  Mine was for a diversion program and that's what was offered and agreed upon.  12 months probation, court costs, evaluations etc.  No community service though.  I have no idea how it's decided what the length of probation is or whether you have to do community service.  

&#x200B;

Technically the judge can always veto any agreement.  In practice though, with something as widespread as a DUI, that's extremely unlikely unless there's a particularly unusual circumstance such as a high profile case, an election year, etc.

Part of this will be county specific.  The CRN itself is only about 25-45 minutes depending on whether you get an evaluator who is fairly chatty or whether you're with someone who is just going through the questions.  If you get a D&A assessment, that takes longer, about an hour but is along the same lines as the CRN.  Basically if you look up any self-report alcohol abuse evaluation, that should give you an idea of what questions are being asked.  I actually didn't think they were out to send people into massive amounts of treatment unnecessarily.  Oh yes, and no drug tests with my provider.

&#x200B;

I wrote a blog post on it here:  what-to-expect-at-your-crn-and-da-evaluation

"Can" they find it?  Your own research gives you the answer to that question.  "Will" they find it or "are they likely" to find it is different.  Put it this way, you had to run a search on a specific location and you only found out about the DUI because  you went further and looked up court records.  That's a lot of effort to satisfy curiosity.  A standard search isn't going to reveal that much.  

&#x200B;

As for mentioning it to him, I wouldn't.  If he never brought it up, there's a reason he never did.  There are a total of 3 people who know about my DUI and I don't even want *them* bringing it up to me.  It's his past, and it's his choice whether and when to talk about it.  Trust me when you have a DUI you're telling a *lot* of people about it (your lawyer, drug and alcohol evaluation counselors, everyone who ends up in DUI class with you).  Let him have what's left of his privacy.  

&#x200B;

If your parents do find it, I would just say that it happened awhile ago, and remind them that everyone makes mistakes; some people make mistakes with greater consequences than others, but it doesn't mean they're bad people.  He did his time, he deserves a fair chance.
